<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Yale J Biol Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Yale Journal of Biology and Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0044-0086</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1551-4056</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">331695</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2595388</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">331695</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Environment and Medicine</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Removal of Atmospheric Particulates by Urban Vegetation: Implications for Human and Vegetative Health</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>William H.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><season>Mar-Apr</season><year>1977</year></pub-date><volume>50</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>185</fpage><lpage>197</lpage><abstract><p>A review of the literature reveals considerable evidence to support the suggestion that vegetative surfaces remove particulate matter from the atmosphere. Preliminary observations of the leaf surfaces of an important urban tree indicate the presence of numerous particulate contaminants. In view of the medical importance of fine particles in urban atmospheres, it is important to assess the efficiency of tree surfaces in particle retention. Can particulate loads be reduced below biologically significant thresholds by vegetation? Are trees acutely injured or subtly influenced in the process of this removal? A brief assessment of research needs is provided.</p><sec sec-type="scanned-figures"><title>Images</title><fig id="F1"><label>FIG. 1</label><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0097-a" xlink:role="192"/></fig><fig id="F2"><label>FIG. 2</label><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0097-b" xlink:role="192"/></fig><fig id="F3"><label>FIG. 3</label><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0098-a" xlink:role="193"/></fig><fig id="F4"><label>FIG. 4</label><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0098-b" xlink:role="193"/></fig><fig id="F5"><label>FIG. 5</label><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0099-a" xlink:role="194"/></fig><fig id="F6"><label>FIG. 6</label><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0099-b" xlink:role="194"/></fig></sec></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0090.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="185" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0091.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="186" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0092.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="187" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0093.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="188" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0094.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="189" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0095.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="190" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0096.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="191" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0097.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="192" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0098.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="193" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0099.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="194" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0100.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="195" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0101.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="196" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="yjbm00137-0102.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="197" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>